A||||5||2birds||Which slave cylinder???||||1||
Z||000000||2birds||08-19-2000||12:03 AM||||I have a 2.8 and Getrag in my 86 GT, and I'm looking for a new slave cylinder.  Advance Auto says I have either a 13/16" or a 15/16".  The 13/16" (in stock) has studs that screw into the cyinder body, and is cast iron, while mine sits on studs that screw into the bracket on the Getrag, and looks like welded tube.  The connections are in the same location, but I assumed mine is a 15/16" and ordered it from Discount Auto ($25 cheaper and I can have it tomorrow instead of Wednesday).  The Discount Auto parts guy said the 13/16" is for the 4 speeds, while the 15/16" is for the Getrag.  Anybody else run into this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-19-2000||01:22 AM||||The Getrag uses the 15/16".....I believe the Isuzu 5-sp. uses the 13/16".<P>That said......DO NOT buy an aftermarket slave!!!   I can't stress this enough.  While it will work for a while (maybe) it will fail eventually.  They are inherently flawed....the cast iron design abosorbs heat and will cause a bleeding nightmare.  Also, I have had problems with the quality.  Several have broken apart on me (the "mast" where the bleeder screw goes crumbles).<P>Spend the extra money and buy a new GM slave (& master for that matter).    It will pay for itself the first time you bleed it.....believe me!  I went through 3 aftermarket slaves in 6 months before I bought a GM one......then the aftermarket master (6  months old) blew out.  <P>My current daily driver is an auto., so I don't have to deal with clutch issues right now, but the next stick shift Fiero I own that needs new clutch hydraulics will get factory GM stuff.  ||||reg||4
Z||000002||2birds||08-20-2000||11:30 PM||||Thanks for the info.  I never thought about heat being a problem with cast iron, since either way, they both sit right next to the head pipe.  15/16" is the correct part.  I priced the GM slave and it was $188, take a week to get it.  I'll probably get the rebuild kit and have my original standing by, but I put on the $60 new Tru-Torque part.  Bled the whole system thoroughly (Gotta love the Mity-Vac).  Fresh DOT 3 makes a diff, too.  Car drives great, now. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||08-21-2000||12:15 AM||||Just an FYI for anyone buying aftermaket slave cylinders......they are all made by the same manufacturer and marketed under different names.  Wagner, Raybestos, Tru-Torque and every other brand sells the same slave cylinder. <P>I guess what I'm getting at is if you must buy an aftermarket slave shop around for the best price. <P>NAPA sells them for $125 and Auto Zone sells them for $65, so there is quite a discrepancy in prices.    <P>2Birds......Let us know how long it lasts (I went through 3 in 6 months.....I hope you have better luck!).<P>BTW....rebuiling slaves is a hit or miss proposition.  Sometimes it works out and sometimes it doesn't.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||08-21-2000||03:06 AM||||<BR>$188(US) for a GM slave!!!!!!?????<P>Highway robbery! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <P>||||reg||4
Z||000005||2birds||08-21-2000||11:05 AM||||$188 is my local dealer's price.  I haven't checked online.  I don't have the luxury of being able to wait for parts like this, since it's my daily driver.  I had the last week of from work, effectively, but now its time to go earn my paycheck.||||reg||1
